cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Harness Forge: a certification authority for fuzzing harnesses"
message: >-
  If you use this software in academic work, please cite it using the metadata
  below. If you cite a number it produced, cite the commit that produced it:
  every row in benchmarks/results/ carries the engine revision that emitted its
  harness, and a coverage figure is only reproducible against a fixed version of
  the thing that measured it.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Obi Ebuka
    family-names: David
    alias: "Obi Ebuka David"
    affiliation: "Department of Computer Science, University of Dayton, Ohio, USA"
repository-code: "https://github.com/eobi/harness-forge"
abstract: >-
  A certification authority for fuzzing harnesses, in which generators are
  replaceable plug-ins. The field builds generators, and the field's own numbers
  say the generator is not the bottleneck: harness defects produce
  false-positive crash rates as high as 94%, and an audit of 586 production
  harnesses found 53 protocol violations. The bottleneck is that nobody can tell
  whether a harness is any good until after it has wasted a campaign or produced
  a false finding. This is not a generator but an intermediate representation, a
  bank of static and dynamic gates, and an evidence record. A producer proposes
  a plan, the gates certify it, and confidence decides nothing. A gate returns a
  verdict with its evidence rather than a boolean, and NOT_RUN is a distinct
  outcome from PASS, so a check that could not run never reads as one that
  passed. Every certificate states what the harness cannot find, computed from
  the sanitizers and knobs in force, so that a campaign reporting nothing has a
  stated scope for its silence.
keywords:
  - fuzzing
  - fuzz-driver-generation
  - harness-synthesis
  - libfuzzer
  - static-analysis
  - software-security
  - memory-safety
  - coordinated-disclosure
license: Apache-2.0
version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-08-29"
